 and  as we have announcements to make we will  certainly  we will certainly inform you  all right  i will now close  this chapter related to  the crisis in afghanistan in foreign  relations in general  and we'll move on to other issues and  maria had a question  maria  hello i hope you can hear me  i would like to come back to the  question i had yesterday for the  commission about the oil spill  coming from syria towards cyprus do you  have any news any update on that thank  you thank you question for tim  hey maria  so uh yeah we we've seen the situation  as badass mentioned to you yesterday and  obviously very concerned about the  potentially serious environmental  impacts um which could come from this  oil spill from a refinery in the syrian  town of benis  we understand that the cypriot  authorities are  following this closely and are ready to  act  cyprus has requested technical  assistance from the european maritime  safety agency through the eu civil  protection mechanism  and an oil recovery vessel from the  agency has been offered to cyprus and  that's currently being mobilized in the  relevancies  the commission would further recommend  that the relevant countries activate the  existing collaboration framework to  respond to oil pollution in the  mediterranean region which comes under  the barcelona convention  and specifically there's a protocol  which concerns preventing pollution from  ships and cases of emergency combating  pollution of the mediterranean sea so  that's what i can say for now on this  issue  thank you very much  any other questions for tim today  on the  energy or environment portfolio  no  any other issues  yes  on polish recovery and resilience plan  uh exactly a month ago the deadline  agreed by european commission and the  push government  on  of assessment of the of the polish  project uh did pass  and  i'm  well aware about the criteria of  assessment so on but my question is  whether the european commercial  commission or poland has asked for the  formal extension of the uh of the of the  finalization of the assessment of the  polish plan thank you  thank you too much there is absolutely  nothing new that i can add compared to  all the previous statements  on this work on the  on the plan continues  there are contacts between of course  the commission and the polish  authorities on the  on the plan that is all that i can say  on this  any other questions for us today  yes please  in the autumn the support for strict  corona measures differs vastly among eu  citizens and according to a new poll  there is a lot of skepticism about the  motivation behind the lockdown in  countries like bulgaria poland and  france  and i was wondering what are the plans  of the commission to unite the people of  europe in the fight against the pandemic  wow  now that is the most open question that  i've had in a very very long time  the plan that the commission has had now  for a very very long time  um has been to ensure  the availability in sufficient  quantities  of  safe and efficacious vaccines  to ensure that the people of europe can  be protected from the virus  and we have been um  extremely focused as you know on  delivering on this plan  and we are now at a stage where i think  it is  very clear to anyone who wants to look  at the evidence  that  the  vaccination campaigns  are providing  a significant degree of safety for  europeans  and therefore  of course we are continuing to cooperate  with countries in particular those where  there is a significant issue of vaccine  hesitancy in terms of informing  the public on the  advantages of  becoming vaccinated and we reiterate on  a very regular basis the call for people  to be uh to be vaccinated so i think  that our efforts have been very clear  for for all to see i think that the  successes  are there but at the same time we  acknowledge  like i think all public authorities that  the efforts must continue  we need to increase the vaccination rate  in particular in those countries where  it is low and we need to continue to  monitor the situation and adapt our  strategy as the epidemiological  situation evolves  thank you very much  any other questions for us on the health  theme  catherine  oops hi eric okay hi eric um  you know somebody asked the question  yesterday about uh the situation about  uneven vaccination across the eu  and um i mean we see very very good  rates and you know it's fantastic to see  how high the rate of vaccination is in  for example ireland  but  uh  you know we also see  the figures for romania and bulgarian  and and yet ireland we here  bought some vaccines from romania a  country that's under vaccinated so  have you got any specific idea of why  romania is not using those vaccines that  it had access to  and  in yesterday's press release uh  commissioner clerici said we will  continue to support those countries that  are  continuing to face challenges so could  you be a bit more specific  and maybe address it in  uh you know in this with a specific  example why is romania selling its  vaccines to ireland when it's so under  vaccinated and ireland is doing  relatively well thank you  thank you catherine for your question  well the first thing i would ask you to  do is to ask  those two countries  to  explain the transactions that took place  between them we are not privy to all of  the individual  discussions that take place between  member states let me just remind you  that vaccines have a shelf life  and therefore  it's obvious that there is an interest  for member states  to ensure  that  vaccines that have that are available in  a country  are used or can be used elsewhere and  this is something that i think we all  have to encourage  in order to ensure that  we maximize the benefits from the  vaccines but for the rest i would ask  you to contact the countries concerned  catherine follow up  so i'm i'm quoting from the commissioner  kiryakiris who said that the eu will  continue to support those countries well  the vaccines has arrived what  specifically does this  sort of support is she referring to  thank you catherine this is a question  we've had uh quite a few times already  and we've said that we are supporting  member states where there are  issues of vaccine hesitancy to  communicate to the public in order to  to reinforce the message on the fact  that vaccines that have been approved  for use within the european union are  safe and efficacious and provide  therefore real benefits for the health  of citizens  and  therefore encourage them to become  vaccinated but let me remind you that  the commission's primary responsibility  is to deliver the vaccines to the member  states vaccination efforts and  vaccination campaigns  are and remain the responsibility  of the national authorities this is  simply  a question of  of a national prerogative  based on the treaties and the  competences of member states  any other questions for us on health  related issues  yes please  yes thanks the commission yesterday  announced that 70 percent of adult  citizens has been vaccinated  um is the commission going to set a new  goal now or  and if not why not  in hindsight was it wise to to set this  goal of 70  back in spring  thank you um  i think that it's abundantly clear  that when the health crisis  was in full swing  at the beginning of the year  the  intent  was to ensure  that we would  explain  very clearly to everyone  that massive vaccination was a must  if we wanted to have a chance of  of coming through this crisis  and therefore setting an objective  focused people's attention  um  it showed that  we were ambitious in terms of delivering  great doses of vaccines to europe and  therefore  it spurred action from all the actors  involved and therefore i think that that  approach has been completely vindicated  the exact percentage  that  that was set at the time was based on  the information available at the time  and i think that with hindsight there  are many things that you can do  and but what is clear is that we have  always said  that we would be very attentive to the  evolutions in the epidemiological  situation  in the pandemic  in the  appearance of variants but at the time  nobody could predict how things would  evolve it is clear today  that um 70  the 70 threshold is definitely a major  achievement but that more needs to be  done  and and therefore that is what we are  now focused on and this is something we  have recognized already a long time ago  since you will remember  that  well before the summer we already  announced that we had signed a  supplementary contract  um with biotech pfizer for the delivery  of a significant number of new doses 1.8  billion in order to continue the  vaccination campaign taking into account  a whole series of needs  be it vaccinating new age categories  within the european population be it  the possible need for booster shots uh  et cetera et cetera so you know we've  always been i think at the same time  very ambitious  but very modest in saying this is based  on what we understand for the moment and  of course we will continue to adapt our  strategy as the situation evolves but  you see the result today  today the result is that  the european union is  one of the  places on earth if i can say where the  vaccination rates are the highest of  course we have issues as have been  highlighted now  of differences in vaccination rates  between countries  but  it is still an extremely significant  success to have reached um in basically  eight months  the um the vaccination reach uh the  vaccination rate that we have reached um  in uh in an eu of 450 million  inhabitants so we are extremely  satisfied by the progress that has been  made but our intent on ensuring that we  continue the efforts as long as required  any other questions for us today on this  that is not the case on other issues  i see no further hand raise so thank you  very much and we will meet again  tomorrow  for the next day briefing  you
 

